@flexiarg cascade-construction/renga-then-semilattice
@title Chain The Practices As A Renga, Then Let The Breaks Make It A Semilattice
@keywords renga, chain, semilattice, ways of working, value chain, breaks, topology, link, shift
@audience cascade builders; anyone relating a set of practices or ways of working to each other
@tone design
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-11-08 (claude-6 at Joe's request; for Joe's review)
@execution LLM or human writing of one verse per step, with the break check by a reader who did not write the chain.
@epistemic-value each break is a finding about the topology that a list or a tree would have hidden.
@references [cascades/declared-skeleton creativity-commons/process-fits-local-conditions]
@see-also cascade-construction/order-by-what-each-step-needs, cascade-construction/separate-construction-from-meaning-review, forward-model/the-forward-model-is-a-pattern-cascade
@how write the practices as a chain of IF/HOWEVER/THEN/BECAUSE verses in which each verse answers only the one before it: its IF takes up the previous THEN, its HOWEVER is the shift. Where no honest IF follows from the previous THEN, stop and record a break (missing link, fork, cycle, a precondition that links to nothing, a participant who never appears). Then rebuild as a semilattice: give each practice the plural upstreams the breaks showed it needs, each with its own because, and fit the practices to each other and to the local conditions.
@violation-signature a chain with no recorded breaks; a break smoothed over by an IF the previous THEN does not supply; a semilattice drawn without the chain that justified its extra edges.
! conclusion: To relate a set of practices, first chain them as a renga, one link at a time, and record where the chain breaks; then rebuild them as a semilattice whose extra edges are the ones the breaks called for.

  + context: A set of declared practices (ways of working) is listed side by side, each with its own probe, and pushed forward together like pawns in a line.

  + IF:
    The practices are meant to support each other, but nothing says how one feeds the next.

  + HOWEVER:
    A list hides dependence, a tree forces a single parent, and a semilattice drawn straight away invents edges no one has tested.

  + THEN:
    Write the practices as a renga, each verse taking up only the previous verse's THEN, so the set reads as a value chain. Record every place the chain cannot continue honestly. Then add the edges the breaks call for, each with its because, giving a semilattice; adjust the practices to fit each other and the circumstances rather than the chain.

  + BECAUSE:
    A renga link can be checked locally, one pair at a time, so its breaks are evidence rather than taste; Alexander's point is that the working structure is a semilattice, and the breaks say where its overlaps are.

  + evidence: Cascade Live, 2026-11-08 (futon3c holes/excursions/RENGA-cascade-live-2026-11-08.md): a seven-verse chain over the ways of working broke five times (rulings not reaching preferences; a fork into software and mathematics; a cycle from capability back to the first turn; viability linking to nothing; other participants never appearing). Joe read the result as "a value chain rather than a set of pawns" and the breaks as the case for "a semilattice and not a chain: we need to modify the ways of working to fit each other and to fit the circumstances".
